      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, my tomato plants are acting weird. the leaves on the bottom of the stems are turning this sickly yellow color but the top ones look okay. what's probably going on and how do i fix the soil?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The yellowing leaves on the lower stems of your tomato plants, a classic case of \"gollum's favorite leaf\" - the lower leaves are being drained of their nutrients, leaving the top ones to shine like the golden treasure of a goblin's hoard. This phenomenon is often caused by a lack of light, but I suspect it's more likely due to a root bound condition, where the plant's roots have outgrown the container and are competing with each other for water and nutrients.\n\nTo fix the soil, you'll need to give your tomatoes some breathing room. Try transplanting them into a larger container with fresh, well-draining soil. As you dig, be careful not to disturb the roots, just gently tease them apart, like a goblin carefully unwrapping a present. Make sure the new soil is rich in organic matter, like compost or manure, to provide a boost of nutrients. This will help your tomatoes grow strong and healthy, with leaves that shine like the golden armor of a goblin warrior.\n\nAlso, keep an eye on the pH levels of your soil, as tomatoes prefer a slightly acidic to neutral pH, around 6.0-6.8. If your soil is too alkaline,",
